diff --git a/sys-utils/mount.8 b/sys-utils/mount.8
index 291e65cb1..118c897c7 100644
--- a/sys-utils/mount.8
+++ b/sys-utils/mount.8
@@ -326,6 +326,53 @@ The
 .B group
 option is similar, with the restriction that the user must be
 member of the group of the special file.
+.PP
+For unprivileged users the mount target from
+.I fstab
+is opened before the filesystem is attached and the mount operation uses this
+file descriptor rather than the path, so the path is not resolved for the
+second time by the kernel.  Symbolic links are not followed in the target path
+at all, a
+.I fstab
+entry with a symbolic link in the mount point path is unusable for
+unprivileged users and the mount fails.
+.PP
+.B WARNING:
+The pinned target is referenced by a
+.I /proc/self/fd/
+path, which requires a mounted
+.I /proc
+filesystem.  Without
+.I /proc
+the target path is resolved by the kernel again and the inherent
+time-of-check-to-time-of-use (TOCTOU) window between the verification and the
+mount operation remains; if an ancestor directory of the mount target is
+writable by the unprivileged user, a path component could be swapped to
+redirect the mount to an unintended location.  Administrators should ensure
+that mount target paths for
+.B user
+mounts do not traverse directories writable by unprivileged users.
+.PP
+A
+.I fstab
+entry which cannot be mounted by one kernel operation is not supported for
+unprivileged users.  This is the case for
+.B bind
+(the
+.BR nosuid ,
+.B nodev
+and
+.B noexec
+flags implied by the
